<BaseLayout title="Early-Universe Gravitational Waves — Jonas El Gammal" description="SIGWAY, LISA, and reconstruction of primordial curvature perturbations.">
  <div class="shell"><header class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">Primordial spectra from induced gravitational waves</span><h1>Early-universe gravitational waves</h1><p>Scalar-induced gravitational waves may let LISA probe curvature perturbations and inflationary dynamics on otherwise inaccessible scales.</p></header></div>
  <article class="prose"><h2>Reconstructing the curvature spectrum</h2><p>Density perturbations large enough to be interesting on small scales generate gravitational waves at second order when they re-enter the horizon. This offers an indirect window onto scales far beyond those measured in the cosmic microwave background, but the observable spectrum is a broad, quadratic convolution of the primordial curvature spectrum rather than a point-by-point image of it.</p><p>As part of the LISA Cosmology Working Group, I study the inverse problem at three levels: agnostic binned reconstruction, physically motivated spectral templates, and first-principles inference of inflationary models. This makes it possible to ask both what a detector can reconstruct without strong assumptions and what it could tell us about concrete early-universe dynamics.</p><ul class="paper-list"><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.11320"><strong>Reconstructing Primordial Curvature Perturbations via Scalar-Induced Gravitational Waves with LISA</strong></a><span>LISA Cosmology Working Group · arXiv:2501.11320</span><p>Compares binned, template-based, and inflationary-model reconstructions of the primordial curvature spectrum.</p></li></ul><figure class="figure"><img src="/assets/img/detector_sensitivity.svg" alt="Sensitivity of gravitational-wave detectors to primordial curvature perturbations"/><figcaption>Detector sensitivity to enhanced primordial curvature perturbations.</figcaption></figure><h2>SIGWAY</h2><p><a href="/software/sigway/"><strong>SIGWAY</strong></a> is the open-source analysis framework behind this work. It separates the primordial spectrum, cosmological transfer kernel, and numerical integrator, so the same inference machinery can combine analytic templates or inflationary solutions with radiation- or early-matter-dominated histories.</p><p>For binned spectra, SIGWAY ships precomputed bilinear coefficients. The induced signal can then be evaluated as a matrix contraction instead of repeating the full double integral. That makes model-agnostic reconstruction fast enough for inference—and powers the interactive laboratory below.</p></article>
